# ⚡ Performance Standards

## Core Web Vitals Targets
- **LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)**: < 2.5s
- **FID (First Input Delay)**: < 100ms
- **CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)**: < 0.1
- **TTFB (Time to First Byte)**: < 600ms

Monitor these metrics in production, not just development.

## Bundle Size Budgets
- **Initial JS bundle**: < 200KB gzipped
- **Initial CSS bundle**: < 50KB gzipped
- **Total page weight**: < 1MB initial load
- **Third-party scripts**: < 50KB combined

Use bundle analyzers to track and enforce these limits.

## Image Optimization
- Use Next.js `<Image>` component (automatic optimization)
- Serve modern formats (WebP, AVIF) with fallbacks
- Properly size images (srcset for responsive)
- Lazy load images below the fold
- Provide width and height to prevent CLS
- Use placeholder blur for better perceived performance

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<Image
  src="/hero.jpg"
  alt="Hero image"
  width={1200}
  height={600}
  priority // for above-the-fold images
  placeholder="blur"
/>

// ❌ Bad
<img src="/hero.jpg" alt="Hero" />
```

## Code Splitting
- Dynamic imports for routes and heavy components
- Lazy load modals, dialogs, and non-critical features
- Split vendor bundles intelligently
- Prefetch critical routes with Next.js Link

```tsx
// ✅ Good - lazy load modal
const Modal = dynamic(() => import('@/components/Modal'), {
  loading: () => <Spinner />,
});

// ❌ Bad - bundle everything
import Modal from '@/components/Modal';
```

## Server Components (Next.js)
- Default to Server Components
- Only use Client Components when necessary:
  - useState, useEffect, event handlers
  - Browser-only APIs
  - Third-party components requiring client
- Keep Client Components small and leaf-level
- Fetch data in Server Components when possible

## Data Fetching Patterns
- Parallel data fetching (avoid waterfalls)
- Cache aggressively with proper revalidation
- Use `loading.tsx` for streaming
- Implement pagination for large lists
- Debounce search inputs
- Use SWR/React Query for client-side caching

```tsx
// ✅ Good - parallel fetching
const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([
  fetchUser(id),
  fetchPosts(id)
]);

// ❌ Bad - waterfall
const user = await fetchUser(id);
const posts = await fetchPosts(id); // waits for user
```

## React Performance
- Memoize expensive calculations with `useMemo`
- Memoize callbacks with `useCallback` when passing to children
- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components
- Virtualize long lists (react-window, react-virtuoso)
- Avoid inline object/array creation in render
- Key lists properly (stable, unique keys)

```tsx
// ✅ Good
const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => 
  computeExpensiveValue(data), 
  [data]
);

// ❌ Bad - recomputes every render
const expensiveValue = computeExpensiveValue(data);
```

## Font Loading
- Use `next/font` for automatic optimization
- Preload critical fonts
- Use font-display: swap to prevent FOIT
- Limit font variants (weights, styles)
- Subset fonts when possible

## Third-Party Scripts
- Load scripts asynchronously
- Use Next.js `<Script>` component with proper strategy
- Self-host analytics when possible
- Lazy load non-critical scripts (chat widgets, etc.)
- Use facades for heavy embeds (YouTube, maps)

```tsx
// ✅ Good
<Script 
  src="https://analytics.example.com/script.js" 
  strategy="afterInteractive" 
/>

// ❌ Bad
<script src="https://analytics.example.com/script.js"></script>
```

## CSS Performance
- Use Tailwind's JIT mode (eliminates unused CSS)
- Avoid CSS-in-JS for critical path
- Inline critical CSS
- Remove unused CSS with PurgeCSS/Tailwind
- Use CSS containment for complex layouts

## Database & API Performance
- Index database queries properly
- Implement query result caching
- Use database connection pooling
- Paginate large result sets
- Use SELECT specific fields (not SELECT *)
- Avoid N+1 queries
- Set appropriate cache headers

## Monitoring
- Track Core Web Vitals in production (Vercel Analytics, Google Analytics)
- Set performance budgets in CI/CD
- Monitor bundle size changes in PRs
- Use Lighthouse CI for automated checks
- Alert on performance regressions

## Performance Checklist

Before shipping:

- [ ] Lighthouse score > 90 (Performance)
- [ ] Core Web Vitals meet targets
- [ ] Images optimized and lazy loaded
- [ ] Bundle size within budget
- [ ] Critical paths optimized
- [ ] Server Components used where possible
- [ ] No unnecessary client-side JavaScript
- [ ] Fonts optimized with next/font
- [ ] Third-party scripts loaded efficiently
- [ ] Database queries indexed and optimized
